      "source_example_id": "e-10889",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating the Derivative of a Function From Its Graph",
      "kp_id": "e-10889"
    },
    "q-344801": {
      "dom_id": "step-q344801",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14252554/assets/34d404457497fea78bd8b4e03d9ab9f4cc270487f1c478cb52780748e52163d5.png)\n\nThe graphs of the functions $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ are given above. Find ${h}^{′}(1)$ where $h(x)=f(x)+g(x).$\n\n${h}^{′}(1)=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "The addition and constant multiple rules for derivatives give\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)={f}^{′}(x)+{g}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(1)={f}^{′}(1)+{g}^{′}(1).\n$$\n\nAlso, notice that $x=1$ corresponds to\n\n- the interval $x\\in (-1,2)$ for the function $f,$ and\n\n- the interval $x\\in (-1,2)$ for the function $g,$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14252554/assets/62c27964999163f87224059d85e5e6fe07a2e8b343e9fef1c34723bf9fbaaec1.png)\n\n- The slope of $y=f(x)$ is constant on $x\\in (-1,2)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=\\frac{1-(-2)}{2-(-1)}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(1)=1.$\n\n- The slope of $y=g(x)$ is constant on $x\\in (-1,2)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δg}{Δx}=\\frac{-2-1}{2-(-1)}=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${g}^{′}(1)=-1.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(1) & ={f}^{′}(1)+{g}^{′}(1) \\\\  & =1+(-1) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
