    "q-104785": {
      "dom_id": "step-q104785",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14252554/assets/df4fed951351dfc1203ba6257f404a5efadbec37b9f11098af1fc36a7793b0c7.png)\n\nThe graphs of the functions $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ are given above. Find ${h}^{′}(2)$ where $h(x)=g(x)-f(x).$",
      "worked_solution": "The addition and constant multiple rules for derivatives give\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)={g}^{′}(x)-{f}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(2)={g}^{′}(2)-{f}^{′}(2).\n$$\n\nAlso, notice that $x=2$ corresponds to\n\n- the interval $x\\in (-1,3)$ for the function $f,$ and\n\n- the interval $x\\in (-1,3)$ for the function $g,$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14252554/assets/814ce0fdce1428b3bd619caa5e8d09a828d2f2c52fd0b20b7f520562319fb848.png)\n\n- The slope of $y=f(x)$ is constant on $x\\in (-1,3)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=\\frac{4-1}{3-(-1)}=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(2)=\\frac{3}{4}.$\n\n- The slope of $y=g(x)$ is constant on $x\\in (-1,3)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δg}{Δx}=\\frac{1-4}{3-(-1)}=-\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${g}^{′}(2)=-\\frac{3}{4}.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(2) & ={g}^{′}(2)-{f}^{′}(2) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
