      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14252554/assets/39431059174ad545642732d57f40689e13af962ed2bc091b86eb0d4f41717299.png)\n\nThe graphs of the functions $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ are given above. Find ${h}^{′}(2)$ where $h(x)=-2f(x)+3g(x).$",
      "worked_solution": "The addition and constant multiple rules for derivatives give\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)=-2{f}^{′}(x)+3{g}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(2)=-2{f}^{′}(2)+3{g}^{′}(2).\n$$\n\nAlso, notice that $x=2$ corresponds to \n\n- the interval $x\\in (1,3)$ for the function $f,$ and\n\n- the interval $x\\in (1,3)$ for the function $g,$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14252554/assets/33082034fa45d3228168431e8263c5a9f2c557ce26a8d637596a1e4e6d47d314.png)\n\n- The slope of $y=f(x)$ is constant on $x\\in (1,3)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=\\frac{2-1}{3-1}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(2)=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n- The slope of $y=g(x)$ is constant on $x\\in (1,3)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δg}{Δx}=\\frac{3-(-1)}{3-1}=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${g}^{′}(2)=2.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(2) & =-2{f}^{′}(2)+3{g}^{′}(2) \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
